`$` is the general directive marker. This page covers template-specific meanings.
For general directive ownership and syntax, see @../directives (Directives).

These template directives are current support. The compiler owns every directive
and formatter under every builder; `$config` is the only source directive. There is
no builder extension point for new directives. See
@../directives (Directives) for their shared rules.

They begin with `$`.

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    content = [$md:
        # A heading

        This body is Markdown.
    ]
]

Common directives include:

- `$slot` for `$slot`, `$slot name` and `$slot 1` declarations
- `$in name:` to fill one named slot
- `$before ...` and `$after ...` to wrap direct children
- `$fresh` to skip one parent wrapper
- `$md` for Moth Markdown
- `$raw` for unformatted text
- `$note` and `$todo` for ignored template notes
- `$html`, `$css`, `$escape_html` and `$code` under every builder

A slot declaration occupies its head alone. Each `$in` directive fills one named
slot. See @../templates/#template-slots (Template slots) and
@../templates/#child-wrappers (Child wrappers) for composition details.

The compiler defines the directive set and executes every formatter. Builders cannot
add, remove or override directives.

A formatter applies only to the template that declares it. Nested child
templates choose their own formatter.
